# Carom — Balls, serving, and physics

This file defines how many balls are in play and how they are served, and how a
ball moves and interacts with the field: the physics loop, collision, and the
paddle bounce and spin mechanic. Every figure below carries the name this
specification gives it.

## The ball

One ball is in play: radius `BALL_R`, serve speed `SERVE_SPEED`, a `SPEED_MULT`
multiplier per paddle hit up to the `SPEED_CAP` cap, and the spin mechanic below.

## Physics

The runtime hands the game the real elapsed time for each frame and imposes no
timestep of its own. Every rate in this specification is integrated against that
delta time: the speeds in units per second, the spin acceleration in units per
second squared, the spin decay per second, and the countdown in seconds.

The outcome of a scenario is independent of how the elapsed time was divided into
frames. The same interval delivered as one long step, as many short ones, or as an
uneven mixture reaches the same state.

The core is render-free: state advances purely from the elapsed time it is handed,
independent of the canvas, of wall-clock time, and of the renderer. Any randomness
runs off the generator seeded from `state.rngState`.

Each frame, for the ball:

1. Apply the spin acceleration (below) to the velocity.
2. Advance position by `velocity * dt`.
3. Resolve collisions with walls, paddles, and obstacles.

## Collision

Resolve the ball against the top and bottom walls, the two paddles, and the two
obstacles. Treat the ball as a circle of radius `BALL_R` and each paddle as an
axis-aligned rectangle `PADDLE_W` by `PADDLE_H`.

- Top and bottom wall: reflect the vertical velocity (`vy -> -vy`) and push the
  ball back inside the field. Speed is unchanged.
- Obstacle: an obstacle bounce leaves speed unchanged and preserves spin, so the
  spin keeps curving the ball after the bounce.
- Paddle: the next section. A paddle hit is the only collision that changes speed
  and imparts spin.

The ball stays inside the field and on the outside of every paddle and obstacle at
every speed up to `SPEED_CAP`.

## Paddle bounce and spin

When the ball strikes a paddle:

1. Reflection angle from contact point. Let
   `offset = (ballCenterY - paddleCenterY) / PADDLE_HALF`, clamped to `[-1, 1]`.
   The outgoing angle from horizontal is `theta = offset * MAX_BOUNCE_ANGLE`
   (`55deg`). Hitting the paddle center sends the ball straight across; hitting
   the top or bottom edge sends it off at `MAX_BOUNCE_ANGLE`.
2. Speed. `speed = min(speed * SPEED_MULT, SPEED_CAP)`. The horizontal direction
   flips to point toward the opposing goal, giving new velocity
   `vx = +/- speed * cos(theta)` (sign toward the opponent) and
   `vy = speed * sin(theta)`.
3. Spin from paddle motion. The paddle's vertical velocity at contact adds spin:
   `spin += paddleVy * SPIN_FROM_PADDLE` (`0.85`), then clamp `spin` to
   `[-SPIN_CLAMP, SPIN_CLAMP]` (`+/-900`). A paddle moving downward as it strikes
   curves the ball one way, a paddle moving upward curves it the other, and a
   stationary paddle leaves the spin as it was.

Spin is a signed scalar carried by the ball, `state.ball.spin`. Each frame it
applies a lateral acceleration perpendicular to the ball's direction of travel, of
magnitude `|spin|` in units per second squared, curving the path toward the side
the sign of `spin` selects. Spin decays toward zero exponentially, losing half its
magnitude every `SPIN_HALFLIFE` (`0.8`) seconds
(`spin *= 0.5 ^ (dt / SPIN_HALFLIFE)` per frame). It persists across wall and
obstacle bounces, and changes through paddle hits and this decay alone.

## Serving

At the start of the match and after each point, the ball spawns at the field
center `(FIELD_CX, FIELD_CY)` and holds there while `state.holdTimer` counts down
from `HOLD_TIME` (`1.0` second) to `0`. On the first frame the timer reaches `0`,
the ball is served at `SERVE_SPEED`:

- The serve travels toward `state.receiver`, the player who was just scored on.
  The first serve of a match travels toward player one (`"left"`).
- The serve leaves at `SERVE_ANGLE` (`12deg`) from horizontal. Whether that angle
  points up or down the field is yours; either way the serve is within
  `SERVE_MAX_ANGLE` (`30deg`) of horizontal.

An elapsed `holdTimer` is the one thing that launches the ball.

## After a point

When a point is scored, the rally ends. The field holds through the countdown at
the center, then the ball is re-served toward the player who was just scored on.
